# Untitled

Designing metal-organic framework fiber network reinforced polymer electrolytes to provide continuous ion transport in solid state lithium metal batteries
Wanqing Fan\(^{1}\), Ying Huang\(^{1}\) (✉), Meng Yu\(^{1}\), Kaihang She\(^{1}\), Jingren Gou\(^{2}\), and Zheng Zhang\(^{2}\) (✉)
\(^{1}\) MOE Key Laboratory of Material Physics and Chemistry Under Extraordinary Conditions, School of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, Northwestern Polytechnical University, Xi'an 710072, China  \(^{2}\) Beijing Key Laboratory of Membrane Materials and Engineering, Department of Chemical Engineering, Tsinghua University, Beijing 100084, China
© Tsinghua University Press 2023
## ABSTRACT
Polyethylene oxide (PEO)-based solid-state electrolytes are considered ideal for electrolyte materials in solid-state lithium metal batteries (SSLMBs). However, practical applications are hindered by the lower conductivity and poor interfacial stability. Here, we propose a strategy to construct a three-dimensional (3D) fiber network of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs). Composite solid electrolytes (CSEs) with continuous ion transport pathways were fabricated by filling a PEO polymer matrix in fibers containing interconnected MOFs. This 3D fiber network provides a fast \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) transport path and effectively improves the ionic conductivity \((1.36\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{S\cdot cm^{-1}}\), \(30\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\). In addition, the network of interconnected MOFs not only effectively traps the anions, but also provides sufficient mechanical strength to prevent the growth of Li dendrites. Benefiting from the advantages of structural design, the CSEs stabilize the Li/electrolyte interface and extend the cycle life of the Li-symmetric cells to \(3000\,\mathrm{~h~}\). The assembled SSLMBs exhibit excellent cycling performance at both room and high temperatures. In addition, the constructed pouch cells can provide an areal capacity of \(0.62\;\mathrm{mA\cdot h\cdot cm^{-2}}\), which can still operate under extreme conditions. This work provides a new strategy for the design of CSEs with continuous structure and stable operation of SSLMBs.
## KEYWORDS
polyethylene oxide (PEO), solid-state lithium metal batteries (SSLMBs), three-dimensional fiber network, composite solid electrolytes (CSEs)
## 1 Introduction
Solid-state lithium metal batteries (SSLMBs) hold great promise as the next-generation energy storage devices due to their high energy density and enhanced safety [1–4]. Among various types of solid-state electrolytes (SSEs) [5, 6], solid polymer electrolytes (SPEs) have garnered significant attention due to their flexibility, low cost, and tunability compared with inorganic electrolytes (IEs) [7, 8]. Notably, polyethylene oxide (PEO) stands out among SPEs for its strong \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) solvation ability and high dielectric constant [9–11]. However, SPEs suffer from poor mechanical properties, low ionic conductivity (IC) and Li dendrite growth in practical applications, which hinder their further development [6, 12]. Since composite solid electrolytes (CSEs) can combine the advantageous features of both SPEs and IEs [13–15], they have become a research hotspot in recent years, making them an ideal choice for SSLMBs electrolyte materials.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), with tunable porosity, abundant Lewis-acid functional groups, and open metal sites, have been considered as promising nanofillers [16, 17]. The Lewis-acid functional groups in MOFs facilitate the dissociation of Li salts. While the open metallic sites can trap the anions, suppress the concentration polarization, and guide the uniform deposition of Li [18–21]. For instance, Lei et al. incorporated zeolitic imidazolate frame work-8 (ZIF-8) into the PEO matrix and increased the ion conductivity and a \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) transfer number to \(2.2\times10^{-5}\;\mathrm{S\cdot cm^{-1}}\) \((30\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) and 0.36, which are much higher than pure PEO [22]. The assembled Li symmetric battery exhibited stable cycling for \(600\,\mathrm{h}\). However, most of these reports show that the MOFs are simply dispersed in the polymer matrix, limiting their ability to guide \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) transport along a continuous pathway, because of inherent nanoparticle aggregation [23, 24]. Additionally, the random distribution of MOFs fails to provide sufficient mechanical strength to suppress Li dendrite growth. Therefore, it is crucial to construct a high-strength interconnected MOFs network that offers continuous pathways for ion transport to enhance PEO-based solid electrolytes [25–29].
