Herein, we have designed a rational structure to solve the above problems. To provide continuous pathways for ion transport, the three-dimensional (3D) MOF/polyvinylidene fluoride-cohexafluoropropylene (PVDF-HFP) interconnected network was prepared by electrospinning MOFs with PVDF-HFP. Subsequently, CSEs were fabricated by infiltrating the MOF/PVDF-HFP network with a PEO polymer matrix solution. The uniform embedding of the 3D fiber network inside the polymer matrix reduced the crystallinity of the PEO and facilitated \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) transport in the PEO matrix. The as-prepared CSEs exhibited
high ion conductivity \((1.36 \times 10^{-4} \, \mathrm{S \cdot cm^{-1}}, \, 30 \, ^{\circ} \mathrm{C})\) and wide electrochemical stability window \((5.32 \, \mathrm{V})\). The MOFs particles tandemly linked on the fibers effectively immobilized anions [30], leading to improve the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) transference number (0.49). In addition, the 3D fiber network significantly enhanced the mechanical strength of the PEO matrix, suppressing \(\mathrm{Li}\) dendrite growth [25]. The well-designed CSEs not only demonstrated stable \(\mathrm{Li}\) plating/stripping for \(3000 \, \mathrm{h}\), but also exhibited excellent cycling performance in SSLMBs. Specifically, the pouch cell assembled based on the CSEs delivered an areal capacity of \(0.62 \, \mathrm{mA \cdot h \cdot cm^{-2}}\) and was able to power light-emitting diode (LED) lights, demonstrating the potential for practical applications. This work emphasizes the necessity of constructing continuous ion transport pathways and provides a feasible strategy for achieving long-term stable cyclic SSLMBs.
## 2 Results and discussion
The 3D fiber network was observed by FESEM. The results showed that the surface of PVDF-HFP fibers was smooth and interconnected to form a network structure (Fig. 2(a)), which provided a good channel for adding PEO/LiTFSI polymer matrix solution filling. The FESEM images of (MOF/PVDF-HFP)x showed that the MOFs are tandem on the PVDF-HFP fibers, but
the morphology is obviously different when different amounts of MOFs are added. Specifically, in Fig. 2(b) \((\mathrm{MOF/PVDF-HFP})_{3}\) a small amount of MOFs were connected in series by PVDF-HFP fibers, which would result in reduced contact between MOFs and the polymer matrix and thus prevented the formation of continuous pathway, which could lead to a reduction in its electrochemical properties. With the increase of MOFs content, MOFs were well connected in series on PVDF-HFP fibers \(((\mathrm{MOF/PVDF-HFP})_{2}\), Fig. 2(c)). When the content of MOFs is further increased, it appeared serious agglomeration on the surface of the fiber \(((\mathrm{MOF/PVDF-HFP})_{1}\), Fig. 2(d)), which would not be conducive to the improvement of electrochemical performance. Figure 2(f) schematically plots the tandem structure on fibers with different MOFs contents. Furthermore, EDS mapping (Fig. 2(e)) showed that C, F, Co, and N elements are evenly distributed in the MOF/PVDF-HFP 3D fiber network, indicating that the electrospinning process combines MOFs with PVDF-HFP very homogeneously. Compared with the uniform surface of PEO/LiTFSI polymer matrix, the polymer matrix connects the fiber network after casting PEO/LiTFSI polymer matrix solution on the fiber network (Fig. 2(g) and Fig. S2 in the ESM). Therefore, more conduction paths are created for \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) transport. The FESEM image of the cross section (Fig. 2(h)) showed that the thickness of PHML is \(56.92 \, \upmu \mathrm{m}\). At the same time, EDS mapping (Fig. 2(i) and Fig. S2(d) in the ESM) also confirmed that C, F, N, S, and Co elements were uniformly distributed in the PHML electrolyte, indicating that the (MOF/PVDF-HFP) fiber network reinforced PEO/LiTFSI/ILs composite electrolyte was successfully prepared. On the other hand, the optical photos of Fig. 2(j) show that the PHML electrolyte has a favorable flexibility enabling it to be bent and folded.
It can be clearly found that PEO electrolyte has two strong peaks at \(19.3^{\circ}\) and \(23.5^{\circ}\) through XRD patterns, indicating that the polymer matrix is highly crystalline and the movement of the molecular segments is hindered resulting in difficult migration of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) and thus a lower IC [33]. The migration of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) in the polymer matrix depends on the continuous complexation and dissociation reactions between lithium and oxygen, and the IC is a critical factor affecting the performance of SSLMBs. Therefore, it is necessary to reduce the crystallinity of the PEO to obtain higher IC. As shown in Fig. 3(a), the introduction of the MOF/PVDF-HFP fiber network reduces the two peaks of the PEO, meaning that the addition of the 3D fiber network inhibits the crystallization of the PEO. In addition, diffractions peak of MOFs has been found in PEO/LiTFSI/(MOF/PVDF-HFP)2 (PHM) and PHML electrolyte, which indicates that MOFs filler is compatible with polymer matrix. The phase transition behavior of the PEO and PHML electrolyte was characterized by differential scanning
calorimetry (differential scanning calorimetry (DSC), Fig. S3 in the ESM). As shown in Fig. 3(b), the melting temperature \((T_{\mathrm{m}})\) of PEO appeared at \(60.2\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . After the introduction of 3D fiber network structure, the \(T_{\mathrm{m}}\) of PHML decreased to \(50.4\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) which further confirmed that the addition of 3D fiber network structure reduces the crystallinity of PEO matrix and facilitated the improvement of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) mobility in the polymer. The dissociation degree of Li salts in CSEs was analyzed by Raman spectroscopy. In the Raman spectrum of PHML (Fig. 3(c)), free \(\left[\mathrm{TFSI}^{-}\right]\) and ion cluster \(\left[\mathrm{Li}[\mathrm{TFSI}]_{2}\right]^{-}\) correspond to two peaks at \(744~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) (F1, yellow region) and \(753~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) (F2, red region), respectively. The percentage of "free TFSI-" can be calculated according to the following equation, and the dissociation degree of Li salts can be expressed as [34]
\[
\mathrm{Free}(\mathrm{TFSI}^{-})=\frac{\mathrm{A_{F1}}}{\mathrm{A_{F1}}+\mathrm{A_{F2}}}\times100\%
\]
where \(\mathrm{A_{F1}}\) denotes the intensity of F1 peak and \(\mathrm{A_{F2}}\) denotes the intensity of F2 peak. The Li salts of PHML electrolyte has a dissociation degree of \(93.5\%\) , which is significantly higher than that of other electrolytes (Fig. S4 in the ESM), indicating that the addition of 3D fiber network promotes the complete dissociation of Li salts and inhibits the formation of ion clusters in the electrolyte [35, 36].
The thermal stability of CSEs was detected by thermos gravimetric analyzer (TGA). Figure 3(d) shows that the introduction of the 3D network structure significantly increased the thermal decomposition temperature of the polymer matrix (from \(250\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (PEO) to \(341\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (PHML)) [37]. Such a high decomposition temperature can fully meet the requirements of SSLMBs for electrolyte safety. Dimensional stability is also another factor to evaluate the stable operation of SSLMBs at high temperatures. Figure 3(e) shows the optical images of the dimensional stability of the electrolyte as a function of
temperature. All electrolytes maintain a relatively stable-state before \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . When the temperature was further increased to \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) PEO melted to a transparent state and PEO/LiTFSI/PVDFHFP (PH) had a slight melting phenomenon. Due to the introduction of the MOF/PVDF-HFP fiber network, PHM and PHML were able to maintain a stable dimensional structure even at \(120\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) which broadens the operating temperature range of SSLMBs. The stress–strain curves (Fig. 3(f)) showed that the tensile strength of PEO was only \(0.48\;\mathrm{\:MPa},\) and the tensile strength of PHML was as high as \(15.06\;\mathrm{\:MPa}\) . Due to the introduction of ILs, the tensile strength of PHML is slightly lower than that of PHM \(\left(16.67\;\mathrm{MPa}\right)\) . The improvement of mechanical properties can effectively prevent the growth of Li dendrites [8]. The above results demonstrate that the introduction of 3D network structure improves the thermal stability and mechanical properties of the polymer matrix comprehensively, thereby reducing the risk of battery short circuit.
The electrochemical performance of the electrolyte was then analyzed. The linear scanning voltammetry (LSV) curves (Fig. 5(g)) showed that the electrochemical windows of PHM \((5.26\,\mathrm{~V})\) and PHML \((5.32\,\mathrm{~V})\) are higher than PEO \((4.38\,\mathrm{~V})\) , indicating that the introduction of 3D network structure and ILs helps to improve the electrochemical stability window of the electrolyte, which can stabilize the polymer chain segment at a relatively high voltage, thereby achieving high electrochemical stability [18, 38]. The IC is a crucial factor for the application of SSLMBs. The investigation of IC found that compared with PEO electrolyte \((9.23\times10^{-6}\;\mathrm{S}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-1},\,30\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) , PHM has a higher IC value \((3.07\,\times\,10^{-5}\,\,\mathrm{S}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-1},\,30\,\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) (Fig. 3(h) and Table S1 in the ESM. Figure S5 in the ESM shows the Nyquist plots of CSEs from 30 to \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) . This indicates that the embedding of the 3D fiber network destroys the crystallization of the polymer matrix, increases the amorphous region, and promotes the motion of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) . The addition of a small number of MOFs reduces the crystallinity of PEO,
enhancing IC. When the filler is further increased, the MOFs crystal aggregates and blocks the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) channel, causing lower IC [15, 25] (Fig. S6 in the ESM). The IC value of PHML at \(30\,^{\circ}\!C\) is \(1.34\times\) \(10^{-4}\ \mathrm{S\cdot cm^{-1}}\), which is four times as much as PHM. This maybe because the plasticizing effect of ILs not only increases the dielectric constant of the system, promotes the dissociation of Li salts and increases the number of carriers, but also weakens the interaction between the PEO segments and increases the mobility of the segments, thereby promoting ion migration [14, 18, 39]. The Li symmetric battery was assembled to evaluate the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) transference number \(\left(t_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}}\right)\) of the electrolyte. As shown in Fig. 3(i), the \(t_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}}\) of PHML is 0.49, which is much higher than that of PEO (0.13, Fig. S7 in the ESM) due to the formation of continuous pathways in the PHML and facilitates faster transport of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\). Moreover, the interconnected MOFs network can capture anions based on its high nanopore structure and abundant open metal sites, which can not only inhibit the concentration polarization of the electrolyte system, but also avoid uncontrollable side-reactions reduced on the electrodes.
The interfacial stability of electrolytes against Li metal was examined by testing the long-term cycle stability of Li symmetric batteries. As shown in Fig. 4(a), the Li/Li battery assembled with PHML provides a stable cycle performance more than \(3000\;\mathrm{h}\). In sharp contrast, the Li/PEO/Li battery has a short circuit around \(500\,\mathrm{h}\) and a large fluctuation in the polarization voltage, while the Li/PH/Li battery with 3D PVDF-HFP fiber can be stably cycled for more than \(970\ \mathrm{~h~}\). In addition, according to the local amplification, it can be seen that the polarization voltage of Li/PHM/Li battery is slightly larger than that of Li/PHML/Li
battery (Fig. S8 in the ESM). The possible reasons for the difference between the above four symmetrical batteries are as follows: (1) 3D MOF/PVDF-HFP fiber network provides a robust framework support and improves the mechanical properties of PHM; (2) MOFs particles in series on PVDF-HFP fibers are uniformly distributed in the polymer matrix, and \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) is uniformly deposited by fixing anions; and (3) the addition of ILs improves the IC and \(t_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}}\) of the electrolyte, which is conducive to further promoting the uniform deposition of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\), inhibiting the growth of Li dendrites and showing more stable Li plating/stripping performance [15, 40, 41]. The Li/PHML/Li battery was used to further evaluate the rate performance of the PHML electrolyte (Fig. 4(b)). When the current densities were 0.2, 0.3, and \(0.5\,\mathrm{\mA{\cdot}c m^{-2}}\), they showed low overpotentials of 58, 87, and \(150\;\mathrm{mV}\), respectively, showing good rate performance.
The Li symmetric batteries with different cycle times were disassembled, and the surface morphology of the Li anode was observed by FESEM. As shown in Fig. 4(c), many small particles were grown on the surface of the Li anode, when the Li/PEO/Li battery was cycled for \(200\ \mathrm{h}\). After \(400\ \mathrm{h}\) of cycling, the small particles further grow to form mossy, and the Li surface was filled with uneven Li deposition. In contrast, the Li/PHML/Li battery has a relatively uniform surface, and still maintains a smooth surface morphology after \(400\ \mathrm{h}\) cycles. Generally, PEO-based polymers are prone to side reactions with Li anode under the action of an electric field, which increase the electrode/electrolyte impedance (Fig. S9 in the ESM), while also destroys the morphology of Li anode, and uneven \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) deposition on the surface to form dendrites. In contrast, \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) can diffuse rapidly in the PHML
electrolyte, which promotes a uniform deposition of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) on the surface of the Li anode. In addition, the 3D MOP/PVDF-HFP fiber network in the PHML electrolyte provides superior mechanical properties and effectively inhibits the Li dendrite growth (Fig. 4(d)). The reasonably designed PHML electrolyte structure extends the cycle life of Li symmetric batteries to \(3000\,\mathrm{h}\), which is superior to most recently reported CSEs (Fig. S10 in the ESM) [15, 18, 26, 42–46].
To further reveal the difference between the behavior of PEO and PHML electrolytes for Li plating/stripping, the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) concentration and electric field distribution at different stages were simulated by COMSOL Multiphysics (Fig. 4(e)) [18, 27]. Due to the lower \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) transference number of the PEO electrolyte, a large amount of free TFSI exists in the PEO, which moves in the opposite direction to \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\), which would inhibit the rapid migration of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) and lead to a larger \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) concentration gradient in the PEO
than PHML. In addition, the depletion of anions (TFSI-) near the Li anode further induces the creation of a space charge layer, leading to a larger potential gradient in the PEO electrolyte. These factors further deteriorate the interface, causing inhomogeneous Li deposition in the PEO electrolyte as well as the growth of dendrites. In contrast, a smaller \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) concentration and potential distribution are generated in the PHML electrolyte, which favors the promotion of dendrite-free Li metal deposition. The interfacial phase between the PHML and the Li anode was further studied by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectroscopy. As shown in Fig. 4(f), the C and O peaks before the cycle are mainly attributed to the PEO polymer matrix, the N peaks are derived from LiTFSI and ILs, and the F, S, and Li peaks are mainly derived from LiTFSI. After \(400\,\mathrm{h}\) cycling, an interfacial phase composed of organic phase (C- \(\mathrm{OCO}_{2}\mathrm{Li}\), ROLi (R represents alkyl group, indicating the formation of lithium-containing organic matter))
and inorganic phase \(\mathrm{(Li_{3}N,}\) LiF, \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{3},\) and \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) ) was detected. A multi-component interfacial phase is reported to be the key to stabilize the electrode/electrolyte compatibility [47]. Among them, LiF is an important component, which has high IC and plays an important role in improving the stability of the electrode/electrolyte interface [48, 49].
To confirm the necessity of constructing continuous ion transport pathways, CSEs were prepared by dispersing MOFs with a mass fraction of \(10\;\mathrm{wt.}\%\) in the PEO/LiTFSI matrix, denoted as PM. The FESEM images show a random dispersion of MOFs in the PEO matrix (Fig. S11 in the ESM). The XRD patterns confirm the reduced crystallinity of the polymer matrix (Fig. S12 in the ESM). Subsequently, we analyze in detail the differences between the electrochemical properties of the electrolytes. Firstly, the IC of the CSEs was compared, as shown in Fig. 5(a). The PM electrolyte exhibits an IC of \(1.77\times10^{-5}\;\mathrm{S\cdot cm^{-1}}\) at \(30~^{\circ}\!C.\) . Due to the reduced crystallinity of the PEO polymer matrix, the IC of the PM electrolyte is better than that of PEO, but significantly lower than that of PHM and PHML. In addition, the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) transference number of PHM and PHML electrolytes were much higher than that of PM (0.28, Fig. S13 in the ESM). These results suggest that the continuous transport path provided by the highly interconnected MOF/PVDF-HFP fiber network in the electrolyte facilitates the rapid \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) migration [25, 50]. Next, Li-symmetric batteries were assembled to analyze the electrochemical stability during Li plating/deposition. Figure 5(b) shows that the assembled Li/PM/Li exhibits a higher polarization voltage with a significantly fluctuating curve, suggesting an unstable interface. This may be due to the inability of the individually dispersed MOFs to provide sufficient mechanical strength to inhibit Li dendrite formation. Furthermore, the lower \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) transference number easily leads to a large Li concentration gradient, resulting in a strong electric field at the interface, which is not conducive to uniform \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) deposition [18]. Finally, the cycling performance of SSLMBs was investigated, and the capacity of the assembled batteries with Li anode and \(\mathrm{LiFePO_{4}}\) (LFP) cathode began to decay slowly after 30 cycles (Fig. 5(c)) and showed a larger polarization potential (Fig. S14 in the
ESM), which indicated poor interfacial compatibility.
In order to intuitively reflect the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) transport in different electrolytes, a detailed schematic diagram is drawn in Fig. 5(d). In the PEO/LiTFSI matrix, the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) transport is disordered and slow due to the high crystallinity of the PEO and the forces between the polymer chain segments that hinder the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) transfer. Although randomly dispersed MOFs can reduce the crystallinity of the PEO, the interfaces between them are loose and lack continuity. \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) must pass through a migration path from the MOFs to the PEO to the MOFs during transport, which limits the IC enhancement. By constructing a continuous MOFs interconnection network, a consecutive interfacial transport channel is provided for \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) transport. However, the migration of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) in the polymeric matrix remains limited. With the aid of IEs, the PHML electrolyte achieves rapid migration in the polymer matrix, thus improving the electrochemical performances of CSEs. Moreover, the 3D continuous network structure provides a strong mechanical support to suppress the growth of Li dendrites. The metallic sites of MOFs can trap anions and guide the Li uniform deposition for reliable interfacial properties [38, 51, 52].
In order to verify the practical application of CSEs, LFP/CSEs/Li batteries were assembled for electrochemical performance test. The galvanostatic charge and discharge curves (GCD) curves (Fig. 6(a)) show the voltage distribution at \(0.2~C.\) The 3D MOF/PVDF-HFP gives the electrolyte higher IC and excellent \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) channel, improving the first discharge specific capacity of the LFP/PHML/Li batteries. As shown in Fig. 6(b), the cycling performance of SSLMBs at \(0.2~C\) was examined. Due to the low IC and poor stability, the LFP/PHD/Li batteries start to decay around 70 cycles, and the specific capacity was only 59.3 \(\mathrm{mA\cdot h\cdot g^{-1}}\) after 100 cycles. Although the introduction of 3D PVDF-HFP improves the structural stability of the electrolyte, the LFP/PH/Li batteries still have a certain attenuation because of the low IC, maintaining a specific capacity of \(87.4\%\) (112.2 mA.h.g-1) after 300 cycles. On the contrary, SSLMBs with PHM and PHML electrolytes exhibit excellent cycling stability due to the construction of a continuous \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) conduction pathway. The
LFP/PHML/Li batteries can still provide a capacity of 152.7 mA·h·g\(^{-1}\) after 300 cycles, which is significantly superior to the LFP/PHML/Li batteries \((137.6\,\mathrm{mA\cdot h\cdot g^{-1}})\), and the Coulombic efficiency has been maintained at \(\sim100\%\). This can be attributed to the presence of ILs in the PHML of accelerated \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) diffusion. Not only that, the LFP/PHML/Li batteries also shows good rate performance (Fig. 6(c)). The LFP/PHML/Li battery delivers high specific capacities of 169.7, 151.3, 142.5, and \(120.1\,\mathrm{mA\cdot h\cdot g^{-1}}\) at 0.1, 0.2, 0.5, and \(1.0\,\mathrm{C}\), respectively. When the current density is restored to \(0.1\,\mathrm{C}\), the capacity can be restored to \(166.6\,\mathrm{mA\cdot h\cdot g^{-1}}\), which is obviously higher than that of LFP/PEO/Li battery (99.2 mA·h·g\(^{-1}\)). It can be found that the specific capacity of LFP/PEO/Li batteries decreases sharply especially at 0.5 and \(1.0\,\mathrm{C}\), which may be attributed to the low \(\mathrm{IC}\) of the PEO, which cannot support its rapid ion transport at high rate. The advantages of the 3D MOF/PVDF-HFP interconnection network for constructing continuous \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) transport paths in polymer matrix are further
confirmed. In addition, interfacial instabilities are also responsible for the capacity decay in LFP/PEO/Li batteries. Figures 6(d) and 6(e) show that the LFP/PEO/Li battery exhibits a higher polarization potential and interfacial impedance than the LFP/PHML/Li, indicating that the PHML has a more stable interface compatibility with the electrodes. The cycling performance of the electrolyte with different MOFs content was also studied (Fig. S15 in the ESM). When the MOFs content was low it was not enough to form a continuous transport pathway, especially the conduction at the interface was insufficient. While the MOFs content was too much, the MOFs crystals aggregated into clusters with each other, which seriously block the ion transport. All these factors that lead to a decrease in specific capacity. Subsequently, the LFP/PHML/Li battery was tested for a long cycle at a high rate (Fig. 6(f)). The LFP/PHML/Li batteries can maintain the specific capacity of \(145.5\,\mathrm{mA\cdot h\cdot g^{-1}}\) \((0.5\,\mathrm{C})\) and \(115.2\,\mathrm{mA\cdot h\cdot g^{-1}}\) \((1.0\,\mathrm{C})\) after 450 cycles, respectively. The
charge/discharge voltage distribution at different cycles also indicated the good stability of the PHML electrolyte (Fig. S16 in the ESM). This indicates that the introduction of 3D MOF/PVDF-HFP and ILs improves the IC and \( t_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}} \), enabling rapid ion transport at high rate and improve electrochemical performance.
Besides, the cycling performance of LFP/PHML/Li battery was detected at room temperature (RT). In order to improve the interfacial contact between the cathode and the electrolyte, a liquid electrolyte of \( 2~\upmu\mathrm{L}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-2} \) was used to wet the electrode on the cathode side of LFP/PHML/Li battery, which significantly reduces the interfacial impedance of the battery (Fig. S17 in the ESM). The LFP/PHML/Li battery can maintain a capacity of \( 89.7\% \) \( (118.2\ \mathrm{mA\cdoth\cdotg^{-1}}) \) after 100 cycles at \( 0.2~\mathrm{C} \), and the coin cell can light up the "NWPU" with the LED lamp (Fig. 6(g)). To illustrate the superiority of PHML electrolyte, the cycle performance of the LFP/PHML/Li battery reported in this work was compared with other SPEs reported recently (Fig. S18 and Table S2 in the ESM). It can be seen that the PHML electrolyte designed in this work is superior to most of the previous reports. The PHML electrolyte, Li strip, and LFP cathode \( (5.0\ \mathrm{mg\cdot cm^{-2}}) \) were constructed as a pouch cell to demonstrate the practicality. As shown in Fig. 6(h), the LFP/PHML/Li battery exhibits a specific capacity of \( 123.6\ \mathrm{mA\cdoth\cdotg^{-1}} \) and an areal capacity of \( 0.62\ \mathrm{mA\cdoth\cdotcm^{-2}} \). In addition, abuse tests were conducted to verify the flexibility and safety of the prepared CSEs. The LFP/PHML/Li pouch cells were able to light up the connected LED lamp even after bending, folding, punching, and cutting, demonstrating the good flexibility and excellent safety (Fig. 6(i)). In summary, the design of PHML electrolyte demonstrates the effectiveness and necessity of constructing continuous ion transport pathways in CSEs with 3D fiber network. The creative design in this work provides a feasible strategy for the practical applications of SSLMBs.
## 3 Conclusions
In summary, CSEs with continuous structure were prepared by electrostatic spinning and solution casting methods. The introduction of the interconnected 3D MOF/PVDF-HFP network not only significantly reduced the crystallinity of the polymer matrix improved IC \( (1.36\,\times\,10^{-4}\,\,\mathrm{S\cdot cm^{-1}},\,\,30\,\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}) \), and provided continuous pathways for efficient ion transport. Furthermore, it also enhanced the mechanical properties of the polymer matrix. Additionally, the MOFs induce the uniform deposition of \( \mathrm{Li^{+}} \) due to the immobilization of anions. Benefitting from the structural advantages, the Li/PHML/Li symmetrical battery exhibited stable cycling for \( 3000~\mathrm{h} \) without encountering any short circuits. The LFP/PHML/Li battery demonstrated excellent cycling and rate performance. Moreover, the assembled pouch cell demonstrated reliable functionality even under extreme conditions such as bending, folding, punching and cutting, thus highlighting its practical value. This study provides an excellent strategy to promote the practical application of SSLMBs.
